Building asusctl for Debian and Ubuntu
======================================

Two build modes are supported, and debian/rules picks between them by looking
for a vendored crates tarball next to the sources. The vendored mode is the one
to use for anything you intend to ship; the plain checkout build is for
convenience and is not reproducible.

Offline, from a vendored tarball (recommended)
----------------------------------------------

Build hosts with no network access — Launchpad builders, sbuild with
--no-network, official Debian buildds — need the dependencies inside the source
package. Generate the tarball from the release tag, including the lock file
cargo produces, and place it in the root of the source tree:

    cargo vendor vendor
    tar cJf vendor_asusctl_<upstream-version>.tar.xz vendor Cargo.lock

    dpkg-buildpackage -b -uc -us

The lock file has to travel with the crates it describes: without it
"cargo build --locked" has nothing to check the vendored sources against and
fails with "requires a lock file to be present first".

This mode is also what makes the build repeatable. The tarball fixes the
dependency versions once, so a source package that built yesterday still builds
today regardless of what has been published to crates.io in the meantime.

Vendor with the same cargo the package will be built with. The resolver honours
rust-version, so a newer cargo can select crates an older one cannot compile.
Beware that upstream ships rust-toolchain.toml pinning "stable", which the
rustup shim obeys; RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN, or calling a versioned binary such as
cargo-1.93 directly, takes precedence over it.

Note that the resolved versions depend on the state of crates.io at the moment
cargo vendor runs, so two tarballs generated minutes apart are not necessarily
identical. Generate one per release and keep it.

From a git checkout (needs network, not pinned)
-----------------------------------------------

    dpkg-buildpackage -b -uc -us

With no tarball present, cargo resolves and downloads dependencies as it
normally would. This is handy while working on the packaging, but it is exposed
to whatever crates.io looks like at that moment: Cargo.lock is gitignored
upstream, so a checkout carries none, cargo writes a fresh one, and
debian/rules removes it again on clean.

That exposure is not theoretical. A dependency that publishes a breaking change
without bumping its major version breaks every unpinned build at once — the
same way it breaks "make build" and the PKGBUILD in distro-packaging, which
also resolve from scratch. When that happens, pin the offending crate
explicitly and vendor the result:

    cargo generate-lockfile
    cargo update -p <crate> --precise <version>
    cargo vendor vendor
    tar cJf vendor_asusctl_<upstream-version>.tar.xz vendor Cargo.lock

debian/rules calls /usr/bin/cargo and sets RUSTC=/usr/bin/rustc explicitly, so
the build uses the distribution toolchain even on a developer machine where
rustup shims come first in PATH. Both are needed: cargo finds rustc through
PATH unless RUSTC is set, and a rustup shim would follow upstream's
rust-toolchain.toml and try to install the components it lists.

Linker
------

Upstream's .cargo/config.toml routes linking through .cargo/linker-wrapper.sh,
which uses mold or lld when either is installed. debian/rules overrides this to
the system linker so that the resulting binaries do not depend on which linkers
the build host happens to have.

Services
--------

asusd.service has no [Install] section: it is activated by udev, through
SYSTEMD_WANTS on the asus-nb-wmi driver. debhelper's generated maintainer
scripts handle the units correctly, and no hand-written postinst or prerm is
needed.

Translations
------------

The compiled message catalogues only exist where the Makefile installs them,
which releases up to 6.3.11 do not. They are therefore picked up in
override_dh_install rather than listed in rog-control-center.install, since
dh_install aborts on a pattern that matches nothing. Generating them requires
msgfmt, hence the gettext build dependency.
